course: dictionary
lesson: 6785
title: Learning a word from its context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are new to you, but the words around them give clues. Read the whole sentence before you decide what a word means. «Every evening an orangutan climbs high, bends branches into a springy platform and piles leaves on top.» Climbing high and bending branches tell you the orangutan lives up in trees.
explain: Writers often compare new things with things you know. «...a bit like you making a den out of cushions» means the orangutan builds a nest, a bit like a den. The comparison helps you picture something you have never seen.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. An orangutan is a large, intelligent ape with long reddish-orange hair and very long arms. Does that fit the clues? Yes, it does.
fact: An orangutan is a large, intelligent ape with long reddish-orange hair and very long arms.
fact: Orangutans live in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra in South-East Asia.
fact: Orangutans spend most of their lives in trees, and they are the largest tree-living animals.
fact: The name «orangutan» comes from Malay words meaning «person of the forest».
fact: Orangutans are critically endangered because their forests are cut down for farms and palm oil plantations.
exercise: «Orangutans spend almost all their lives in trees.» Where do orangutans mostly live? | In cities | *In trees | In deserts | In the sea
exercise: «Their arms are far longer than their legs.» What does this tell you about an orangutan's arms? | *They are much longer than its legs | They are the same length as its hair | They are shorter than its legs | They have no arms at all
exercise: «Orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra.» Which two places are their home? | Australia and Japan | *Borneo and Sumatra | Scotland and Wales | London and Paris
test: «Orangutans are critically endangered.» What does this suggest? | Orangutans are very common in every country | Orangutans live in deserts | *Orangutans are at very high risk of dying out | Orangutans are fast runners
test: «Orangutan babies stay with their mothers for about 7 or 8 years.» What does this show? | Babies can climb before they are born | *Babies need their mothers for a long time | Babies leave their mothers at once | Mothers never look after babies
exam: Which clue in «an orangutan climbs high, bends branches into a springy platform» tells you where it lives? | *Climbs high | A bit like you | Evening | Den
